  "description": "The best of three worlds: TensorFlow's way of describing a network, PyTorch's way of running it, and ML.NET's way of learning from a table. Deep learning in C#: the layer over the engines that already exist. Describe, train and use a network in C# — layers, losses, optimizers and a training loop with early stopping and checkpoints, in Keras's words or written as code — while the arithmetic runs on .NET's own vector maths, behind a seam a heavier engine such as libtorch through TorchSharp can stand behind without a line of your model changing. And a table that a tree learns better than a network does not have to become one: the same prepared data is meant for ML.NET's trainers too. DeepSharp provides what an engine does not: the model's shape, the path your data takes into it, the training loop, the checkpoints and the charts.",
